            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Five factors require a fourth power of a prime; three factors require a square of a prime. Equate the resulting expressions for ",
                "marks": []
              },
              {
                "type": "math",
                "latex": "n",
                "display": false
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ".",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "An integer with five positive factors is $p^4$, and one with three positive factors is $q^2$, where $p$ and $q$ are primes. Thus\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{n}{9}&=p^4\\\\n&=9p^4\\\\\\frac{n}{16}&=q^2\\\\n&=16q^2.\\end{align*}$$\nEquating the expressions for $n$ gives\n$$9p^4=16q^2.$$\nThe right-hand side is even, so $p$ is the even prime, $2$. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}q^2&=\\frac{9\\times2^4}{16}\\\\q^2&=9\\\\q&=3\\\\n&=16\\times3^2\\\\n&=144.\\end{align*}$$\nCheck: $\\frac{144}{9}=16$ has factors $1,2,4,8,16$, and $\\frac{144}{16}=9$ has factors $1,3,9$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
